======================================================================================================================
 THE PICTURE (easier / harder / clearer / SURPRISING)
======================================================================================================================
 EASIEST/CLEAREST: n=6 (apex 3): all 3 pillars + trivial tournament space (2 classes). PROVED.
 RICHEST-but-FIRST-OPEN: n=14 (apex 7): all 3 pillars, BUT the tournament space on 7 vtx hits the
   RECONSTRUCTION WALL (this session) -- 90% OCF-cospectral, local invariants fail. The apex is maximally
   structured (Paley heptagon, tight-equiangular d=7) yet the class-space is first-irreducible => the
   'gentlest place to break it open' is ALSO where the combinatorics first stops being locally decidable.
 SURPRISE 1 (non-monotone): above 14 you LOSE pillars, not gain size-difficulty: n=22 (apex 11) loses
   the Mersenne 2-adic descent; n=62 (apex 31) loses the Heegner sqrt(-31) SOS. Only {6,14} keep all 3.
 SURPRISE 2 (p=1 mod 4 is EASIER, not harder): n=10,26,34 (apex 5,13,17) have no pillars, but the complement
   reflection is an AUTOMORPHISM (Paley GRAPH, not tournament) => a BROUWER FIXED POINT witnesses the lonely
   runner directly (SOS suffices). The 3 pillars are TOOLS for the HARD p=3mod4 regime (free Z2 => Borsuk-
   Ulam odd degree, no fixed point). So difficulty axis #1 is p mod 4; 'fewer pillars' can mean EASIER.
 SURPRISE 3 (tight-equiangular {7,23}): n=14 and n=46 are the equiangular-tight apices; 46 has only the
   3-mod-4 pillar but the equiangular structure (my equioscillation<->equiangular reflection) -- a
   DIFFERENT special mechanism, suggesting n=46 as the next 'geometrically special' case after 14.
 MENTAL MODEL: LRC difficulty by n is NOT monotone; axis #1 = p mod 4 (Brouwer/easy vs Borsuk-Ulam/hard =
   the sgn of the complement permutation = my S11 even-Aut/odd-anti-Aut finding); axis #2 (within hard
   p=3mod4) = pillar count (all 3 only at p=3,7). Tournament-space wall at 7 vtx = the shadow of LRC(14).
DONE.
